### Why Did We Choose This Hardware and Software? (Technical Justifications)

#### Hardware Preferences
*   **ESP32:** Thanks to its Dual-Core architecture, it handles internet/MQTT communication and light dimming (PWM) without blocking each other. It also contains a specific hardware module (LEDC) that provides flicker-free light control.
*   **BH1750 Sensor:** Instead of reading noisy and meaningless analog voltages like old LDRs, it sends the ambient light directly in "Lux" units and as digital packets (I2C).
*   **IRLZ44N MOSFET:** It is the electronic valve that protects the system from catching fire. The weak ESP32 operating at 3.3V cannot directly drive a powerful LED drawing 12V/5A. The MOSFET uses that tiny 3.3V signal coming from the ESP32 to open and close the path for the actual main power. To prevent it from heating up, it is driven with PWM logic (Full Open / Full Close thousands of times a second) instead of "Half open".
*   **12V Power Supply (Adapter):** The ESP32 is just the brain; this adapter is the water tank that carries the actual power (muscle).
*   **LM2596 Voltage Step-down:** While the main circuit operates at 12V, this module takes that 12 Volts and drops it to 5 Volts to feed the ESP32 so it doesn't burn out.

#### Software and Architecture Preferences
*   **C++:** The core language used to program the brain of the ESP32.
*   **HTML/CSS/JS:** Forms the user interface (UI) of the system. Building it web-based instead of writing a separate application allows the device to be accessed from any screen.
*   **MQTT Protocol:** Instead of the website sending commands directly to the light, it communicates using a lightweight and incredibly fast Broker. This architecture makes it very easy to add other smart home appliances to the system in the future.
*   **Wokwi:** The platform that allows us to do logic PWM tests via an online virtual simulation without the risk of burning anything until we get the physical hardware.

